.TH T 1 "2026-10-01" "Tend 1.1.0" "Tend manual"
.SH NAME
t, tend \- a calm task manager that tells you what to do next, and why
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B t
.br
.B t
.I command
[\fIarguments\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR]
.br
.B t
.I any text to capture as a task
.SH DESCRIPTION
Tend shows one task at a time and says why it picked it. Three rules decide, in order: a hard deadline at risk comes first; then one task a day from the goal furthest behind its weekly target; then everything else by (value + urgency) / size.
.PP
Run t alone for interactive mode, where every command is one key. Anything that is not a command is added as a task. Every command prints JSON with \-\-json.
.SH DOING
.TP
\fBt next\fR [\-\-low | \-\-high] (key n)
The one thing to do now.
.br
\-\-low or \-\-high says how much energy you have right now (see energy windows).
.TP
\fBt focus\fR [id] [\-\-pomo | \-\-flow | \-\-box 45] (key f)
A focus timer on it.
.br
pomo: 25 minutes of work, then a 5 minute break. flow: counts up with no forced stop; press b for a break sized to the time you worked. box: a hard stop after the given minutes. Keys while it runs: p pause, d done, q stop.
.TP
\fBt start\fR [id] (key b)
Mark it started (focus does this too).
.br
Part of the 'states' feature.
.TP
\fBt done\fR [id] (key d)
Mark it done.
.TP
\fBt skip\fR [id] (key s)
Skip it for today, no questions asked.
.TP
\fBt split\fR [id] ["step" ...] (key x)
Break it into smaller steps.
.br
Without steps, Tend asks for them one by one. Make the first one tiny.
.TP
\fBt wait\fR [id] [date] (key h)
On hold, waiting for someone.
.br
The task leaves the queue. With a date, it comes back that day by itself.
.br
Part of the 'states' feature.
.TP
\fBt drop\fR [id] [\-\-once | \-\-stop] (key k)
Let a task go.
.br
For a repeating task, \-\-once skips just this one and \-\-stop ends the series.
.TP
\fBt status\fR [id] todo|started|waiting|done|dropped
Set any state.
.br
Part of the 'states' feature.
.TP
\fBt undo\fR (key u)
Undo the last change.
.SH CAPTURING
.TP
\fBt add\fR <text> [details] (key a)
Capture a task (or just: t <text>).
.br
Anything that isn't a command is added as a task. Details: see `t help` under 'Adding details'.
.TP
\fBt triage\fR (key t)
Sort the inbox, 3 quick questions each.
.br
For each new task: how much it matters, how big it is, and a date if it has one.
.TP
\fBt edit\fR [id] <details or new title> (key e)
Change a task.
.br
Example: t e 12 due:fri v:3. A word that isn't a detail becomes the new title.
.SH DECIDING
.TP
\fBt resolve\fR (key r)
Decide what to do with slipped tasks.
.br
A task slips when its date passes. Each one needs one decision: today, a new date, split or drop.
.TP
\fBt review\fR (key v)
Weekly review, about 5 minutes.
.br
Seven steps: last week, your patterns, inbox, slipped, waiting, old tasks, goals. q stops anywhere.
.br
Part of the 'review' feature.
.SH LOOKING
.TP
\fBt ls\fR [\-\-all] [\-\-low | \-\-high] (key l)
The queue, in order, with the reason for each.
.br
Shows the first 20 tasks; \-\-all shows every one. Waiting tasks are listed at the end.
.TP
\fBt plan\fR [\-\-ics] (key p)
Today's schedule, built with the same rules.
.br
Books focus blocks around your calendar. \-\-ics prints the plan as a calendar file.
.br
Part of the 'planning' feature.
.TP
\fBt gantt\fR [\-\-days 14] (key c)
The next days as a chart.
.br
Part of the 'planning' feature.
.TP
\fBt goals\fR [add <name> <weekly time> [why] | rm <name>] (key g)
Goals and weekly progress.
.br
A goal with a weekly target gets one 'big rock' task a day (rule 2). Example: t g add thesis 3h
.TP
\fBt wins\fR [\-\-week] (key w)
What you got done today.
.TP
\fBt stats\fR (key i)
How your plans usually go: time, dates, push\-backs.
.br
The median of your last 20 finished tasks. Corrections start after 5 tasks of a kind.
.br
Part of the 'learning' feature.
.SH YOUR DATA
.TP
\fBt backup\fR
Save a copy of your data now (one is made every day).
.TP
\fBt restore\fR [number] [\-\-yes]
List copies, or go back to one.
.br
Your current data is saved first, so a restore can be undone with another one.
.TP
\fBt export\fR [file]
All data as JSON lines.
.br
Without a file, the data goes to stdout and nothing else is printed.
.TP
\fBt import\fR <file | \-> [\-\-replace] [\-\-yes]
Load an export.
.br
Into an empty database: an exact copy. Otherwise the tasks are added, and tasks you already have are skipped. \-\-replace swaps all your data for the file. A copy is saved first.
.SH SETUP
.TP
\fBt notify\fR [\-\-install | \-\-uninstall | \-\-test | \-\-dry\-run]
Send new reminders.
.br
Run by a timer: \-\-install sets up a systemd user timer (Linux) or a launchd agent (macOS). Each reminder is sent once, and none outside the working day.
.br
Part of the 'reminders' feature.
.TP
\fBt features\fR [on | off <name> ...]
Turn optional parts on and off.
.br
The core is always on. A feature that is off disappears; its data is kept.
.TP
\fBt plugins\fR
List plugins and hooks.
.br
Part of the 'plugins' feature.
.TP
\fBt help\fR [command] (key ?)
All commands, or one in detail.
.TP
\fBt doctor\fR
Check the setup, with a fix for each problem.
.br
Checks the config, the database, backups, calendars, hooks, plugins and reminders. Changes nothing.
.TP
\fBt completion\fR bash | zsh | fish
Shell completion script.
.br
bash: add eval "$(t completion bash)" to ~/.bashrc zsh: add source <(t completion zsh) to ~/.zshrc, after compinit fish: t completion fish > ~/.config/fish/completions/t.fish
.SH ADDING DETAILS
Words become the title; these set fields, anywhere in the line:
.TP
\fB+goal\fR
Goal.
.TP
\fBdue:\fIdate\fR, \fBaim:\fIdate\fR, \fBafter:\fIdate\fR
Hard deadline, soft target, hidden until. Dates: today, tom, mon to sun, +3d, +2w, oct20, 10\-20, 2026\-10\-20, 1st, eow, eom. none clears.
.TP
\fBv:\fR1..3, \fBs:\fRS|M|L, \fBe:\fItime\fR
Value (nice to have, matters, really matters), size, estimate (45m, 2h).
.TP
\fB@high\fR, \fB@low\fR
Energy the task needs.
.TP
\fBevery:\fIrule\fR
Repeat: day, weekday, mon,thu, 3d, 2w, month, year, 1st, 15th, last. Only one copy exists at a time.
.SH FEATURES
Everything after version 0.1 can be turned off with t features off NAME:
.TP
\fBreview\fR
weekly review and its reminder (since 0.2).
.TP
\fBlearning\fR
corrections learned from your history, and t stats (since 0.2).
.TP
\fBplanning\fR
t plan, t gantt and calendar import (since 0.3).
.TP
\fBhooks\fR
scripts that run after events (since 0.4).
.TP
\fBplugins\fR
t\-<name> programs run as t <name> (since 0.4).
.TP
\fBenergy\fR
energy windows and @high / @low tasks (since 0.5).
.TP
\fBstates\fR
started and waiting states (since 0.6).
.TP
\fBrepeat\fR
repeating tasks (every:mon) (since 0.8).
.TP
\fBreminders\fR
desktop reminders from a timer (since 0.8).
.SH FILES
.TP
\fI~/.config/tend/config.toml\fR
Settings, each with a comment.
.TP
\fI~/.local/share/tend/tend.db\fR
Your data: one SQLite file.
.TP
\fI~/.local/share/tend/backups/\fR
A copy of the data for each of the last 7 days.
.TP
\fI~/.config/tend/hooks/\fR
Hooks: on_add, on_done, on_drop, on_skip, on_status, on_focus_start, on_focus_end, on_review, on_remind, on_change.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\fBTEND_DB\fR, \fBTEND_CONFIG\fR, \fBTEND_HOOKS\fR
Use another database, config file or hooks folder.
.TP
\fBNO_COLOR\fR
No colors. The plain theme also uses only ASCII.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 for a usage error or a problem t doctor found, 2 for a config file that can't be used.
.SH SEE ALSO
https://github.com/alexhergomz/Tend
